{"title":"Tree Appreciator","link":"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Oct/14/tree-appreciator/","date":1792012981000,"content":"\n    \n        <p><strong>Tool:</strong> <a href=\"https://tools.simonwillison.net/tree-appreciator\">Tree Appreciator</a></p>\n        <p>Another game experiment with Claude. This one used the default Claude iPhone app (chat mode, not Claude Code mode), where I prompted Claude Opus 5.5 Max with:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><code>Build a three js game called \"Tree appreciator\" - it shows you two algorithmically generated trees and the game mechanics is about appreciating the differences between them. Think hard about how to make sure it is both fun and beautiful.</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Somewhat embarrassingly given <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/Sep/27/2026-in-llms-so-far/#simon-willison-2026-in-llms.059.webp\">my recent talk</a>, I've found that telling Opus 5.5 \"make sure it is fun\" works uncomfortably well. In this case Claude <a href=\"https://claude.ai/share/ee7de555-f85c-4ff0-96cb-d51ed5e479e9\">crunched for 3 hours and 7 minutes</a> before retuning a cosy little spot-the-difference game with music and animation and achievements that's quite compelling to play.</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a mobile spot-the-difference puzzle game with a dark interface. Header reads &quot;No. 003 Acer autumnalis ‘Hid…&quot; with subtitle &quot;Autumn maple · A country lane in late a…&quot; and icons for sound, a book, and help. Two nearly identical 3D-rendered scenes are stacked vertically, labeled &quot;Fig. i&quot; and &quot;Fig. ii&quot;, each showing a dark-trunked maple tree with red, orange and pale peach leaves, leaves falling through the air, a golden grassy field with a few small rocks, and hazy hills with pine silhouettes under a pale sky. Two differences are circled in blue in both images: circle &quot;a&quot; at the base of the trunk, where Fig. i has a small red mushroom that is missing in Fig. ii, and circle &quot;b&quot; in the right side of the canopy, where Fig. i has a patch of green leaves that are red in Fig. ii. The bottom bar shows &quot;2 of 4&quot;, a reset button, a &quot;Hint&quot; button with three filled and two empty dots, and a score of &quot;1,803&quot;.\" src=\"/media/b2429d86a91d32ee5958f4db790dbe01dd0383973c6d46946d82509808bc0583.webp\" /></p>\n    \n    \n        <p>Tags: <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/game-design\">game-design</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/javascript\">javascript</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/ai\">ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/generative-ai\">generative-ai</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/llms\">llms</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/claude\">claude</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/vibe-coding\">vibe-coding</a>, <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/tags/three-js\">three-js</a></p>\n    \n\n","author":"Simon Willison","siteTitle":"Simon Willison’s Weblog","siteHash":"1b599216bd27a00f15d67a3ed9719c64148630c70244b6dd8e743f17cb45527e","entryHash":"2bd003ab1bf4d2403477f689732202601db1fbb6f76f3654853d909d8b70305a","category":"Tech"}